t there is  also a reality that sometimes those  preferences can be culturally ingrained  through discriminatory or racist  messages that are sometimes reinforced  by society and culture so for example  some countries have caste systems and  those caste systems can pervade so  strongly that people believe that  something is merely a casual preference  about physical appearance for example  but it's actually ingrained  multi-generationally by teaching that  people of a certain caste are dirty or  toxic or to be avoided or whatever and  it's important to understand that you  might think I just happen not to be  attracted to X and X could be a racial  characteristic or it could be a physical  characteristic of some other kind or  whatever the case may be and you may not  even have an awareness that it is  something cultural that has been  ingrained that is actually responsible  for that does that mean that it should  be enforced that even if you don't find  certain races attractive you should have  to date them of course not  of course not we're not talking about  that we're just trying to have a more  nuanced than deep understanding of where  some of these preferences can come from  in the United States - you know the  caste example is not super relevant in  the u.s. in the u.s. attitudes towards  interracial relationships do affect  attitudes about other races sexually  which can translate to someone thinking  I just happen not to be attracted to  someone from a certain race but it could  be connected to historical  discrimination and taboos about  interracial marriage which fortunately  are less prevalent in 2019  than they were 50 years ago but still to  some degree exist  I know people who tend to date only  outside their race I know both men and  women that are heterosexual and I know  gay men all of whom have dating  preferences that are for those who  are not part of their racial group it  could be by chance it could be connected  to something in their upbringing who  knows so that's the analysis in a  literal sense sexual racism is a thing  and I apologize for hitting my  microphone there is this the type of  systemic racism that is endemic of is is  a prototypical of the problems in  society no it's not and I'm not saying  that and that's where I think the  broader analysis comes in the reality is  that making questions of racism about  something like sexual preferences is  very often a distraction sometimes used  in bad faith by people who either want  to create fake drama around dating  preferences or to use acceptance for  dating preferences on the basis of race  to justify further segregation there is  a case that some on the internet like to  make that listen if it's okay  to only want to date people of certain  races then what's the big deal with more  segregation like why don't we live  around people of our own race and the  next thing you know you're talking about  something like what Richard Spencer has  proposed a peaceful segregation on the  races of base so it's important I'm glad  to address these questions as if they  are good faith questions and I believe  with peace and love that Ken was asking  it in good faith but I would be doing  you a disservice if I didn't tell you  that these types of questions are often  used by bad faith actors to either trick  or induce fruitless or false debates  that are really a distraction from the  issues that as a society we're trying to  solve if you disagree with me let me  know I can't wait to hear from you
 

